#Q2: FILE CARVING / RECOVER DELETED DATA

##Definition
File carving is the process of recovering files from raw disk data by searching for known file header and footer byte signatures, without relying on file system metadata that may have been deleted or corrupted.

##Steps (Foremost)
1. Acquire a forensic image of the disk with dd.
2. Verify the image hash before starting work.
3. Run Foremost against the image. It scans for known file signatures.
4. Foremost writes recovered files into an output directory sorted by file type (jpg/, pdf/, doc/, etc.).
5. Open recovered files and check metadata for case-relevant information.

##Commands
```bash
Install Foremost
sudo apt install foremost

Create disk image
dd if=/dev/sda of=/home/examiner/disk_image.dd bs=4M

Verify image integrity before carving
md5sum /home/examiner/disk_image.dd

Run file carving
foremost -i /home/examiner/disk_image.dd -o /home/examiner/recovered_files/

View what was recovered, sorted by type
ls -la /home/examiner/recovered_files/

Read the audit log Foremost generates
cat /home/examiner/recovered_files/audit.txt

Check EXIF metadata on recovered images (counterfeit document case)
exiftool /home/examiner/recovered_files/jpg/00000001.jpg
```

##Commands (Autopsy GUI)
```
1. Open Autopsy > New Case > Enter case name and examiner name
2. Add Data Source > Disk Image or VM File > browse to disk_image.dd
3. Enable "File Carving" in the ingest modules list > Finish
4. Wait for analysis to complete
5. Go to File Views > Deleted Files in left panel
6. Click any file to view content and metadata
7. Right-click > Extract File to save it
```

##File Signature Table (memorise at least 4)

| File Type | Header (Hex) | Footer |
|---|---|---|
| JPEG image | FF D8 FF | FF D9 |
| PDF | 25 50 44 46 (%PDF) | 25 25 45 4F 46 (%%EOF) |
| PNG image | 89 50 4E 47 | AE 42 60 82 |
| ZIP / DOCX / XLSX | 50 4B 03 04 | 50 4B 05 06 |
| MP3 audio | FF FB | (no standard footer) |

##For Counterfeit Document Case
- Recover deleted image and PDF files from suspect's drive.
- Check EXIF metadata of recovered images: tool used to create/edit (e.g., Photoshop), GPS coordinates, timestamps.
- Look for multiple save dates on the same file (evidence of repeated editing).
- Look for tool signatures: Photoshop leaves "Adobe Photoshop" in EXIF Creator field.

##Key Concepts
- File carving vs file recovery: Carving uses byte signatures only, ignores file system entirely. Recovery uses MFT (NTFS) or FAT entries to locate deleted files. Carving works even when the file system is damaged or wiped.
- Slack space: Unused space between the end of a file and the end of its allocated disk cluster. Can contain remnants of previously stored data.
- Anti-forensics: Suspects may use tools like Eraser or CCleaner to overwrite deleted files and defeat carving. Look for evidence of wiping tools in registry and logs.

##Viva
- Q: What is a file signature? A: A fixed sequence of bytes at the start (and sometimes end) of a file that identifies its type. Also called a magic number. Foremost uses a config file of known signatures to search for.
- Q: Can Foremost recover files from a formatted drive? A: Yes, if the formatting was a quick format (file system table cleared but data blocks not overwritten). Not after a secure wipe.
- Q: What tool would you use for a mobile device image? A: Autopsy can analyse Android images. Cellebrite UFED is the commercial standard for mobile acquisition and carving.

